{Image Size Testing.} In this problem, we are given $N$ numbers in $[R]$ and $0<\gamma<1$, and must decide if every range item appears at least once or if at most $\gamma\cdot R$ range items appear at least once. 
We show for any $\gamma > 0$, the problem has approximate degree and quantum query complexity $\tilde{\Omega}(\sqrt{n})$. 
This holds as long as $N=c\cdot R$ for a certain constant $c>0$.

This matches the upper bound of~\cite{juntatesting}, resolving the main open question from their work. 



  \@startsection{paragraph}{4}
  {\z@}{2ex \@plus 3.3ex \@minus .2ex}{-1em}
  {\normalfont\normalsize\bfseries}
{Statistical Distance From Uniform $(\mathsf{ SDU})$.} In this problem, we are given $N$ numbers in $[R]$, which we interpret 
as a probability distribution $p$, where $p_i=f_i/N$, the fraction of times $i$ appears.
The goal is to compute the statistical distance between $p$ and the uniform distribution to error $\varepsilon$.

This problem was studied by Bravyi, Harrow, and Hassidim \cite{bravyi}, who gave an $O(\sqrt{n})$-query quantum algorithm approximating the statistical distance between
 two input distributions to additive error $\varepsilon=\Omega(1)$.
We show that the approximate degree and quantum query complexity of this task are $\tilde{\Omega}(\sqrt{n})$, even when one of the distributions is known to be the uniform distribution.

This matches the upper bound of Bravyi et al.~\cite{bravyi} and answers the main question left open from that work. Note that the classical query complexity of this problem is $\Theta(n/\log n)$~\cite{VV11}.



